Canonical Principle

J-Hooks / Cross-World Soundtrack Projection: Decompose a musical work into high-level dramatic functions and relational invariants; release its protected surface expression; map those functions into a different narrative world; and reconstruct an original musical system native to the target. Treat motifs as semantic hyperlinks, silence as structured information, and scoring as navigation through a space of narrative-musical relationships.

And:

Audience of One: Once narrative function and musical rendering are separated, the same underlying story can support different original musical realizations adapted to individual listeners while preserving artistic intent, transparency, and user agency.

The trick isn’t:

“Make this movie sound like that movie.”

It’s:

\boxed{\text{Discover why the music worked there, then discover what performs that function here.}}

Classic for a reason.

Extract the reason.

Then make another classic.